{
  "analysis": "The review describes an unintended in-game purchase caused by a double tap, indicating that the purchase interface allows accidental spending. It also describes the game locking the account after the refund and making payment of the refunded amount a condition for regaining access, which functions as a payment wall blocking continued play. The account lock is presented as an intentional game or account rule, not as a bug or technical fault. No external search is needed because the mechanics are clear from the review.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "accidentally brought a pack when I double tapped the screen",
      "rule_applied": "Accidental Purchases indicator",
      "rationale": "The purchase was triggered unintentionally by a double tap, showing that the interface makes it easy to spend money accidentally."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "now my game has been locked and they are holding my account to ransom until I pay the equivalent of the refund I got",
      "rule_applied": "Pay Wall indicator; payment directly blocks continued access to the game",
      "rationale": "The game is made inaccessible until the player pays money, so payment is required to continue playing."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a slot-game mechanic with chance-based wins and compares the cost of playing or buying within this game against other slot options. The wins are also described as varying in frequency, which supports a variable-reward structure. The review does not describe a previously purchased item losing value, a purchase interface reducing friction, or a spending threshold reward, so Power Creep, Easy to Purchase, and Never-Ending Lure are not supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_Gambling",
      "span": "If you are considering slot games there are less costly options with more wins",
      "rule_applied": "Gambling: using money or a paid stake to participate in a chance-based game for a possible prize.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer compares the monetary cost of slot-game options with the chance-based wins they provide."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "The wins are not as big, or as often.",
      "rule_applied": "Variable Rewards: unpredictable or variable rewards used to sustain engagement.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes slot-game rewards varying in both payout size and frequency, identifying the reward-delivery pattern."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a competitive multiplayer dice game whose outcomes are allegedly predetermined rather than random. It says the game selects the winner at the beginning and then distributes favorable or unfavorable rolls to different players, including repeated 6s for one player and mostly 1s or 2s for another. This is an explicit claim that the game manipulates outcome determination, which matches Illusion of Control. The reference to which player wins and the contrasting outcomes for different players also describes competition between players. The predictable-roll complaint is not Optimism and Frequency Biases because it alleges deliberate manipulation rather than merely perceiving frequent near-wins or lucky outcomes. No web search was needed.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "P_IllusionOfControl",
      "span": "Dice rolls are not random at all, they are preplanned. Game randomly decides at the start of the play that which player has to win and dice rolls according to it.",
      "rule_applied": "vs Alleged rigging: assign where the span asserts that the game conceals or manipulates the determination of outcomes, including fixed or predetermined results.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer explicitly alleges that the game predetermines the winner and tunes subsequent dice rolls to produce that outcome."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "which player has to win",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The span describes a game outcome in which players compete against one another for a winner."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a fragmented downloading design: after the player believes the game file has already been downloaded, the game requires another additional download, obscuring the true download requirements. The later matchmaking problem is explicitly attributed to a Wi-Fi problem and is therefore a technical fault rather than a deliberate game mechanic under R11. The review does not clearly describe competition itself, only matchmaking failing.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "Tech_FragmentedDownloads",
      "span": "I thought I already download the file but I still get an additional download",
      "rule_applied": "Definition of Fragmented Downloads: designs that obscure the true storage or download requirements until the game starts; matches the indicator for additional mandatory in-game downloads.",
      "rationale": "The player expected the initial download to be sufficient but is required to download another file, showing that the game's actual download requirements are revealed incrementally."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a concrete storage-capacity problem: the board becomes gridlocked because too many new items accumulate, and archiving those items still consumes inventory space. This is an active capped-inventory shortfall, not merely a complaint about clutter. The energy statement only describes depletion and gives no regeneration timer or paid refill, so it does not establish Playing by Appointment or Pay to Progress. Gems are mentioned only as an in-game resource, without real-money conversion. The ad mismatch concerns external marketing and is out of scope under R13. No collection compulsion, repetitive task, daily reward, or other coded mechanic is explicit. No web search was needed.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_WasteAversion",
      "span": "game is now gridlocked. Way too many new things on a tiny board. Ridiculous. Update: they finally started \"archiving\" but expect you to use your inventory space for them???",
      "rule_applied": "Capped inventory forces disposal or inventory expansion; the Waste Aversion boundary requires a realized capacity shortfall or loss, which is shown by the board becoming gridlocked while archived items still occupy inventory space.",
      "rationale": "Accumulating items exhaust the available board and inventory capacity, leaving the game unable to accommodate them without sacrificing or expanding storage."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes automatic in-game advertisements appearing after every over and every wicket, which interrupts normal gameplay and requires the player to encounter ads repeatedly. The comments about wasting data and time are evaluations of that ad mechanic, not separate mechanics. The catch outcomes describe perceived unfair gameplay but do not explicitly establish rigging or a hidden outcome system.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "Too much ads are shown after every over and every wicket fell.",
      "rule_applied": "definition: compelled to view advertisements; forced ad views encountered within the game",
      "rationale": "Ads automatically appear at recurring gameplay events, making the player repeatedly encounter them during normal play."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes hero acquisition as requiring many repeated fights: after close to 100 fights, the player still has only the two starting heroes, which indicates repetitive activity tied to progression rather than a general complaint that the game is slow. It also explicitly describes an in-game \"share to unlock\" prompt, meaning the player is asked to share or post something as part of unlocking a hero. The reported failure of the unlock does not erase the underlying share-gated mechanic. No payment, daily schedule, collection compulsion, or exit penalty is described.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "I have done close to 100 fights and still have the first two heros you get when you start the game",
      "rule_applied": "Grinding definition and the boundary versus Slow Progress: assign when repeated activity is required or performed for progress.",
      "rationale": "The player describes performing nearly 100 repeated fights while making little or no progress toward unlocking additional heroes."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FriendSpamImpersonation",
      "span": "share to unlock this hero",
      "rule_applied": "Friend Spam / Impersonation boundary: assign when the player is prompted to send or post through a social channel.",
      "rationale": "The phrase describes an in-game prompt requiring the player to share or post something to unlock a hero."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes reaching the game's current maximum normal-level content, then being placed into Champions League while waiting two weeks for another release. On March 2, 2026, only about 50 new levels appeared before the player again reached the endpoint, and by March 3 another Champions League cycle had replaced further normal levels. Each batch is exhausted and the endpoint recedes, matching Infinite Treadmill. The external search established that Toon Blast's Champions League places players in a league with other users, so it is a competition mechanic. ([peakgames.helpshift.com](https://peakgames.helpshift.com/hc/en/4-toon-blast/faq/25-what-is-the-champions-league/?f=what-is-the-champions-league&l=en&s=events&utm_source=openai)) The statements that this is a money grab and that the reviewer lost their bankroll express dissatisfaction and spending but do not specify a payment mechanic, so no monetary label is supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_InfiniteTreadmill",
      "span": "Get new levels on 3/2/26 only to go maybe 50 levels and no more levels.",
      "rule_applied": "definition: content is released in batches that the player exhausts, leaving no attainable completion as the endpoint continually recedes.",
      "rationale": "The player quickly reaches the end of each level batch and must wait for more content, showing a continually extending game rather than a completable endpoint."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "another champions league",
      "rule_applied": "definition: the game makes players compete against other players; the official Toon Blast Help Center identifies Champions League as a league with other users. ([peakgames.helpshift.com](https://peakgames.helpshift.com/hc/en/4-toon-blast/faq/25-what-is-the-champions-league/?f=what-is-the-champions-league&l=en&s=events&utm_source=openai))",
      "rationale": "Champions League is a named competitive league in which players compete against other users."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": true,
  "search_query": "Toon Blast Champions League competition mode",
  "search_result": "The official Toon Blast Help Center establishes that Champions League places players in a league with other users and lasts until new levels are released. ([peakgames.helpshift.com](https://peakgames.helpshift.com/hc/en/4-toon-blast/faq/25-what-is-the-champions-league/?f=what-is-the-champions-league&l=en&s=events&utm_source=openai))"
}

{
  "analysis": "The review distinguishes the game's in-app design from its external advertising. The complaint that the ads are more fun describes third-party marketing and is out of scope under R13, so it does not trigger Advertisement. Inside the game, the reviewer says the gameplay consisted of repeatedly clicking upgrades, conducting research, and moving to locations, portraying progress as a monotonous task loop. The mention of relying on daily quests does not establish a daily login incentive, resetting reward, or penalty for missing a day, so Daily Rewards is not supported.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "all the game ever had me do what click to upgrade this or research that or go there",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Grinding applies where the review identifies repetitive or tedious activities required for game progress.",
      "rationale": "The reviewer describes the game as an ongoing loop of repetitive upgrade, research, and navigation tasks rather than skill-based play."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a referral reward that grants a champion and silver, implying that the game incentivizes referrals and player recruitment. It also describes repeated in-game promotional pop-ups appearing at login; these are direct purchase solicitations, and the \"limited time\" framing uses artificial scarcity. After champions reach level 30, the reviewer explicitly characterizes progression as a grind, indicating repetitive and tedious effort. Finally, the statement that p2p is the best way to win describes payment as providing a gameplay advantage. The pop-ups are promotional purchase offers rather than forced third-party advertisements, so T_Advertisement is not assigned. No web search was needed because the mechanics are clear from context.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Grinding",
      "span": "once your champions hit level 30 it becomes a horrible grind",
      "rule_applied": "definition: performing repetitive and tedious tasks in order to make progress in a game",
      "rationale": "The reviewer names a grind beginning at level 30, implying that advancing afterward requires repetitive, tedious play."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_DeceptiveLuxury",
      "span": "\"special\" or \"limited time\" deals",
      "rule_applied": "Artificial Scarcity: limited-time offers with unnecessary urgency",
      "rationale": "The deals are framed as limited-time offers, using scarcity and urgency to influence purchases."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_EasyToPurchase",
      "span": "Every single time you log in you are bombarded with pop ads advertising \"special\" or \"limited time\" deals",
      "rule_applied": "Prompted to Buy; boundary rule: offers that interrupt a session",
      "rationale": "Repeated promotional pop-ups appear at session entry and directly solicit purchases before play."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "p2p is the best way to win this game",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win: patterns that grant advantages to players who spend money",
      "rationale": "The reviewer states that paying is the best route to winning, implying a paid gameplay advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_ForcedFellowship",
      "span": "got a free champion and silver from a referral",
      "rule_applied": "Social Pyramid Scheme: patterns that reward players for inviting friends or making referrals",
      "rationale": "The game grants valuable rewards through a referral, incentivizing players to recruit others."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

