raw:
{
  "analysis": "The review describes a capped board or inventory space that makes storage insufficient and pushes the player to purchase additional capacity with diamonds, with the price increasing after each purchase. This matches Waste Aversion because the capacity limit is actively operating and the player must spend premium currency to remedy it. The energy spent obtaining needed items does not by itself establish grinding, a wait timer, or pay-to-progress, and the review does not describe diamonds being bought with real money.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_WasteAversion",
      "span": "The limited space is bothersome too and you have to use diamonds to buy more storage with each purchase the price gets higher.",
      "rule_applied": "Capped inventory forces you to destroy items or upgrade inventory; boundary rule requiring a realized capacity shortfall or paid inventory upgrade.",
      "rationale": "Limited board capacity is actively constraining play and forces the player to purchase more storage with premium currency, at an escalating cost."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes two current in-game mechanics. First, the game requires the player to watch an advertisement in order to claim a daily reward, which is an ad-gated reward. Second, the game provides a daily reward, a recurring incentive tied to daily return. The phrase about paid popups reports frequent purchase solicitation, but it does not clearly describe a specific friction-reducing purchase interface such as a default purchase, insufficient-funds prompt, or missing confirmation, so M_EasyToPurchase is not assigned. No external search was needed.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "ask u to watch an ad just to claim a daily reward",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Advertisement covers forced ad views or ads required to obtain rewards.",
      "rationale": "The player must watch an advertisement to receive an in-game reward."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "claim a daily reward",
      "rule_applied": "Daily Rewards indicator: daily login or daily reward incentives.",
      "rationale": "The game offers a reward on a daily cycle, encouraging recurring return to claim it."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game advertisement appearing after every over, making the ad an imposed interruption rather than an external store or marketing ad. It also describes the app closing and requiring a restart when the player accidentally taps the ad, but that behavior is attributed to bad app behavior or a technical issue, so it does not independently support a dark-pattern label. The requests to stop the ads or fix the UI are hypothetical changes and are not coded.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "After every over, an ad pops up.",
      "rule_applied": "definition; compelled to view advertisements within the game",
      "rationale": "An advertisement is imposed during gameplay after every over, describing a forced in-game ad interruption."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes three active mechanics. First, item recharges and cooking require the player to wait before playing more, which is a wait-to-play timer. Second, the game requires ad viewing to obtain a small recharge, so advertisements are compulsory for restoring play capacity. Third, the board has limited space, forcing the player to delete items that represent time already invested; this is realized wasted value from an inventory cap. The statement that the game is a money grab is evaluative and does not independently establish a monetary label.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "the time it takes to recharge items and how long it takes to 'cook'",
      "rule_applied": "Wait to Play: in-game timers that make players arbitrarily wait for something, including resource regeneration or cooking/build timers.",
      "rationale": "Item recharging and cooking impose real-time waits before the player can continue playing."
    },
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "I have to watch adds for a measly recharge",
      "rule_applied": "Forced to watch ads or given rewards for watching ads.",
      "rationale": "The player must watch an advertisement to receive a recharge."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_WasteAversion",
      "span": "I run out of space on the board and have to delete items I spent time on",
      "rule_applied": "Capped inventory forces the player to destroy items; assign when the capacity limit causes realized loss of invested value.",
      "rationale": "The board capacity limit forces the player to discard items on which they have already spent time."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes payment-gated progress: the player says they cannot do anything and cannot complete the card packs or mini games without paying real money. It also describes a collection objective, namely completing card packs, which supports Complete the Collection. The energy cap limits progress through an order, but the review does not state a regeneration timer or required waiting period, so it does not independently support Playing by Appointment. General claims that the game feels like a trap do not establish a specific purchase prompt or interface manipulation.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "you will never be able to complete the card packs and other mini games without being forced to pay real money for it",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Pay Wall",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly states that completing game content is impossible without paying real money."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_CompleteTheCollection",
      "span": "complete the card packs",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The player describes pursuing completion of a card collection."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes a daily login streak that rewards continued daily returns and is reset or lost when the required login condition is not met. The reviewer says the 53-day streak was lost despite logging in yesterday, so the loss is attributed to a malfunction, but under R11 the underlying streak-reset design remains codable as Daily Rewards.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_DailyRewards",
      "span": "I had a login streak of 53 that I just lost today for no reason. I did not miss yesterday's login.",
      "rule_applied": "T_DailyRewards definition; R11 preserves the underlying daily streak penalty when a malfunction or erroneous detection triggers it.",
      "rationale": "A login streak is a daily-return incentive with a streak-loss consequence, even though the reviewer says it was reset erroneously."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review explicitly describes a pay-to-win mechanic: money provides an advantage, and the divide between strong and weak players can only be overcome by spending more. It also describes competitive game modes in which stronger players steal from weaker players. That is ordinary player-versus-player competition, while the asymmetric theft and benefit to stronger aggressors additionally support Encourages Anti-Social Behavior.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "pay2win",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win indicator",
      "rationale": "The term directly states that spending money grants players an in-game advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "game modes where strong players steal from weaker players",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The described game modes make players compete against one another."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_EncouragesAntiSocialBehavior",
      "span": "strong players steal from weaker players",
      "rule_applied": "Boundary rule vs Permitted harm",
      "rationale": "The span describes an asymmetric harmful act in which stronger players take resources from weaker players, implying a gain for the aggressor."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes an in-game schedule that requires repeated logins every few hours and continuous app presence around Arms Race and treasure digs. This imposes game-defined appointments and creates pressure not to miss event opportunities. It also describes competition as being determined by spending, which supports both ordinary Competition and the monetized-rivalry form of Pay to Progress. The statement that the game is a gacha game explicitly names a chance-based reward structure, supporting Reward Mania; however, the review does not explicitly connect real-money spending to the gacha mechanic, so Gambling is not assigned. The claims about false advertising, screenshots, and chargebacks concern marketing or out-of-app conduct and are excluded under R13.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_PlayingByAppointment",
      "span": "login every 2-3 hours 24/7",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The game requires the player to return on a schedule set by the game rather than play only when they choose."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_FearOfMissingOutFOMO",
      "span": "keep it open all day long so you don't miss treasure digs",
      "rule_applied": "definition; boundary rule versus Playing by Appointment",
      "rationale": "The player is pressured to remain active because stopping could mean missing an event or its rewards."
    },
    {
      "label": "M_PayToProgress",
      "span": "Competing=who spends the most.",
      "rule_applied": "Pay to Win / Monetized Rivalries indicator",
      "rationale": "Competition is portrayed as being won through greater spending, encouraging money to be used for in-game status or advantage."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "Competing=who spends the most.",
      "rule_applied": "definition",
      "rationale": "The review explicitly describes players competing against one another."
    },
    {
      "label": "P_RewardMania",
      "span": "This is a gacha game",
      "rule_applied": "Gacha mechanics like pulls, rolls, banners. [indicators]",
      "rationale": "Calling the game a gacha game identifies a chance-based reward-delivery mechanic."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes repeated in-game pop-up screens that interrupt play. In particular, a quit-confirmation screen must be closed with an X before the player can continue. The weekly challenge score popup alone does not establish competition, scheduling, or a reward mechanic. The increased cost is too vague to establish a payment-related label. The required dismissal of the quit screen is an interface obstruction, so it supports UI Misdirection.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_UIMisdirection",
      "span": "are you sure you want to quit screens that you have to X out of before continuing",
      "rule_applied": "UI Misdirection boundary rule: assign where the interface obstructs an opt-out, close, cancel, skip, or exit, including a screen the player must dismiss before continuing.",
      "rationale": "The game forces the player to dismiss a quit-related screen before returning to play, obstructing the exit control through the interface."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The review describes long-term investment in troop and defense upgrades being devalued by later balancing changes: the player spends months building them up and then they are nerfed. It also describes a player-versus-player attack structure in which other players can destroy even a maxed account. The mentions of bugs, sales, nonexistent support, updates, and the game being a chore are general complaints without a qualifying mechanic. Different reward amounts across accounts indicate perceived unfairness but do not by themselves establish a variable-reward engagement system, and reduced times for less-active players do not clearly match a listed schedule or retention mechanic. No external search is needed.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "M_PowerCreep",
      "span": "You will spend months upgrading troops and defenses just to be nerfed.",
      "rule_applied": "definition; Power Creep requires an acquired or invested game element to lose value after later balancing changes or updates.",
      "rationale": "The player's months of investment in upgraded troops and defenses are made less valuable when those upgrades are subsequently nerfed."
    },
    {
      "label": "S_Competition",
      "span": "I have a maxed out account and anyone can destroy me.",
      "rule_applied": "definition; the game makes players compete against other players.",
      "rationale": "The span describes other players being able to attack and destroy the reviewer's account in a player-versus-player structure."
    }
  ],
  "invoked_web_search": false,
  "search_query": null,
  "search_result": null
}

{
  "analysis": "The reviewer describes in-game advertisements and pop-ups appearing sequentially before meaningful gameplay, delaying access to play until the player has endured the cascade. This is an in-game ad-interruption mechanic, not merely a complaint about external marketing. The review does not state that the pop-ups are purchase prompts, use rewards to incentivize ad viewing, or impose a timer-based wait.",
  "labels": [
    {
      "label": "T_Advertisement",
      "span": "seemingly endless in-game ads and pop-ups that cascade one after another so much that by the time I can actually play",
